  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1242 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\n12Z upper air analysis places a 594 dm upper level ridge over\nportions of the Southern Plains and Lower Mississippi Valley. A\nfew subtle shortwaves are traversing the periphery of the upper\nlevel ridge over portions of Oklahoma and Missouri. Late morning\nsurface analysis showed a surface high near the Mississippi Gulf\nCoast and a weak quasi-stationary boundary located just north of\nthe Mid-South over the Middle Mississippi Valley. Southerly winds\non the back side of high pressure are advecting higher surface\ndewpoints in the 70s into the region with 11 AM temperatures\nmostly in the lower 80s.\n\nShort-term model trends suggest the aforementioned stationary\nboundary will drift south into the area this afternoon before\nretreating back north as a warm front later tonight into Thursday\nmorning.  Regional WSR-88D radar trends over the past few hours\nhave shown continued weakening and dissipation of rain showers as\nthey entered the Mid-South. Poor low to mid-level lapse rates and\nweak shear will offset surface based instability rising at or\nabove 2000 J/kg along and north of I-40. Isolated to scattered\nshowers and perhaps a few thunderstorms are possible this\nafternoon. However, severe thunderstorms are not expected at this\ntime due to the less than favorable parameter space.\n\nRecord warmth is expected to continue into Thursday as\ntemperatures will remain well above normal in the lower 90s.\nConfidence remains high for a stronger northern stream upper-\nlevel trough rotating through the Lower Mississippi and Tennessee\nValleys late Thursday night into Friday, pushing a strong cold\nfront through the forecast area. This frontal passage is expected\nto be mostly dry with model soundings showing a relatively dry\nmoisture profile. Much cooler fall temperatures are expected\nbehind this front with highs struggling to reach 70 degrees on\nSaturday and lows dropping into the upper 30s to lower 40s\nSaturday night. We'll be monitoring the potential for some patchy\nfrost over portions of West Tennessee late Saturday night as\nwinds are anticipated to become light with NBM probabilities of\ntemperatures below 38 degrees averaging between 30-40 percent.\n\nLong-term ensemble and deterministic model trends continue to\ndepict dry weather continuing well into next week as an upper-\nlevel ridge builds across the region. Temperatures will gradually\nincrease back above normal into the 80s across many locations by\nthe middle of next week.",
